.*.lock
.ledger.lock
.insights.lock
# The ledger's append lock at its FAIL-OPEN anchor (#348). The NORMAL lock sits in the git
# common dir (`<git-common-dir>/opencrabs-ledger.lock`), inside a directory no working-tree
# walk can reach; when git cannot be run at all (fork exhaustion, a git-less fixture)
# `tools/ledger.py` anchors the lock beside the ledger instead, and THAT file lands inside a
# declared directory -- so the tree declares its name.
opencrabs-ledger.lock
__pycache__/

# The derived ledger index: disposable, regenerable, ~2.9x the text.
.ledger-index.sqlite

# THE GATE-SELECT KILL-ROW STORE IS FACTORY DATA, NOT A SHIPPED ARTIFACT (#644, route A).
# A kill row records a RUN ON A HOST, not a property of the tree: a fresh clone has no runs,
# so it should report no derived kills, and shipping one host's run records makes the tree
# assert things about runs that never happened there. It shipped once, and the tree carried
# 86 rows naming scripts that exist in no tree (95% probe noise from ad-hoc runs that did not
# pin OC_SUBPROCESS_DIR) against 4 naming real gates — a ratio that worsens with every proof
# run, because the store is append-only and a noise row can never be removed.
#
# The invariant it must hold — "a row that ships in the tree names a gate that exists in the
# tree" — is held here BY CONSTRUCTION: nothing ships. The alternative route (keep it tracked,
# gate the invariant post-hoc against a declared baseline) was rejected because its only
# remedy for a violation is WIDENING the baseline, which is the drift-guard-disarmed-by-the-
# act-that-silences-it class. Precedent for a factory-data surface that deliberately never
# ships: `docs/hygiene-protected.json`, whose SHIPPED form is only the skeleton
# `docs/hygiene-protected.example.json` — the live file is absent from every tree by design,
# and its absence is the shipped state (`tools/hygiene.py:137` resolves it; the stated-skip
# readers are `tests/ledger_boundary.py:164` and the citation legs). So this rule's sibling is
# not a tracked file a reader can open; it is the example plus the absent live copy.
#
# The rows already committed are PRESERVED — on disk byte-for-byte, and in git history at
# 5ff312d7 and earlier. `git rm --cached` removed them from the INDEX only; nothing was
# deleted or rewritten to make this rule true.
evidence/subprocesses/
